                        <field name="dc_description">This copy of the "Prognosticum futuri saeculi" by Julian of Toledo is significant to textual history, as it is one of the first comprehensive and systematically constructed eschatologies in Christian literature. It was produced in the Abbey of St. Gall during the last third of the 9th century. Includes glosses, among them some by the hand of Ehhekart IV., and at the end of the manuscript a short note about the works of the St. St. Gall monk Ekkehart IV. (d. 1060; by Ildenfons of Arx entitled "Crisis Ekkehardi IV.") </field>
